From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ieee1905: prefer direct neighbours when stamping a CMDU sender

i1905_link_lookup_partial() returns the first entry that matches, without
looking at whether the link is direct. The neighbour list can hold several
entries for one peer address: one learned from the peer itself, and one from
another device's topology report -- which carries that reporter's AL address,
not the peer's.

cmdu_input.c uses this function to decide who sent a received CMDU. It first
sets rxf->aladdr to the frame's source address, which is correct, and then
overwrites it with the AL address of whatever link the lookup returned. When
that is the indirect entry, every frame from one agent is attributed to
another.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-28 on a three-node mesh where both agents were
associated to the same backhaul AP-MLD, so their traffic arrived on
ap-mld-2.staN interfaces of the controller:

  ap-mld-2.sta1
    nbr_macaddress 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b  nbr_ieee1905id e6:a9:...  direct=false
    nbr_macaddress 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b  nbr_ieee1905id ca:8e:...  direct=true

The indirect entry came first and won. All 372 notifications from that agent
reached map-controller with the other agent's AL address, which then filed its
radios, MLD configuration and metrics under the wrong node -- the mesh looked
like it kept losing track of a device that was in fact healthy and connected.

Note the macaddr test cannot separate the two interfaces either: .sta1 and
.sta2 inherit their MAC from the AP-MLD and are identical.

Walk the list twice and take a direct neighbour if there is one. Behaviour is
unchanged wherever only one entry exists for a peer.

--- a/src/i1905_dm.c
+++ b/src/i1905_dm.c
@@ -407,15 +407,42 @@
 							   uint8_t *r_aladdr)
 {
 	struct i1905_neighbor_interface *ifpeer = NULL;
+	int pass;
 
-	list_for_each_entry(ifpeer, &iface->nbriflist, list) {
-		prlink("Lookup link partial ", ifpeer->macaddr, iface->aladdr, ifpeer->r_macaddr, ifpeer->aladdr);
+	/* Two passes: a direct neighbour wins over one we only know second-hand.
+	 *
+	 * The list can hold several entries for the same peer address -- one
+	 * learned from the peer itself (direct) and one from somebody else's
+	 * topology report, which carries that reporter's AL address. Returning
+	 * whichever comes first then hands back a foreign AL address, and
+	 * cmdu_input.c uses exactly this call to stamp the sender of every
+	 * received CMDU.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-28, three-node mesh with both agents
+	 * associated to the same backhaul AP-MLD. ap-mld-2.sta1 held:
+	 *
+	 *   nbr_macaddress 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b  nbr_ieee1905id e6:a9:...  direct=false
+	 *   nbr_macaddress 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b  nbr_ieee1905id ca:8e:...  direct=true
+	 *
+	 * The indirect entry came first and won, so all 372 messages from that
+	 * agent were attributed to the other one and the controller filed its
+	 * radios, MLDs and metrics under the wrong node. Both .staN interfaces
+	 * share one MAC (they inherit it from the AP-MLD), so the macaddr test
+	 * below cannot separate them either.
+	 */
+	for (pass = 0; pass < 2; pass++) {
+		list_for_each_entry(ifpeer, &iface->nbriflist, list) {
+			if (pass == 0 && !ifpeer->direct)
+				continue;
 
-		if (hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->macaddr, iface->macaddr) &&
-		    (hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->aladdr, r_aladdr) || hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->r_macaddr, r_macaddr))) {
-			ifpeer->tsp = time(NULL);
-			timer_set(&ifpeer->staletimer, I1905_IFLINK_AGEOUT);
-			return ifpeer;
+			prlink("Lookup link partial ", ifpeer->macaddr, iface->aladdr, ifpeer->r_macaddr, ifpeer->aladdr);
+
+			if (hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->macaddr, iface->macaddr) &&
+			    (hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->aladdr, r_aladdr) || hwaddr_equal(ifpeer->r_macaddr, r_macaddr))) {
+				ifpeer->tsp = time(NULL);
+				timer_set(&ifpeer->staletimer, I1905_IFLINK_AGEOUT);
+				return ifpeer;
+			}
 		}
 	}
 
